    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nVolvo EX30 and EX30 Cross Country EVs Recalled for Detaching Head Restraint Cushions\n14 photos\nPhoto: Volvo\nThere's no mistaking that Volvo built its reputation on protecting occupants in crash scenarios. A recall about a head restraint cushion, therefore, certainly deserves attention. The Swedish automaker is recalling a grand total of 9,930 vehicles over this concern, with Volvo Car USA highlighting that said cushions may come off during severe crashes.\nDoes a headrest cushion make a world of difference in a severe crash? Volvo says it does, for it provides vital support to the occupant's head exactly when maximum support is required. The NHTSA calls this campaign 26V671 and lists two model years, namely 2025 and 2026, with production dates ranging from March 3, 2023, all the way through August 28, 2026.\nAlthough the cushion per se is not defective, attachment strength isn't good enough. Volvo's investigation determined that certain head restraint cushions are likely to come off because of insufficient fastening. The recall filing describes the crash that exposes the fastening issue, specifically high-speed scenarios combined with impacts from multiple directions.\nThat particular combination of variables increases the risk of a cushion separating from the head restraint structure. Thankfully for Volvo's legal team and customers alike, Volvo Car USA is not aware of any report alleging injuries (or worse) connected with the recall condition.\nNot as fortunate, the recall condition offers no warning before a crash. The head restraint and its cushion look and function just as intended in everyday use, with the defect revealing itself only when the battery-electric vehicle is subjected to the multi-directional loading that severe crashes produce. China-based Magna Seating Baoji is listed as the supplier of the cushion.\nPhoto: Volvo Volvo started looking into this problem back in September 2025. Both the automaker and the supplier began separate investigations, assessing the existing design to come up with a more robust revision. One year later, Volvo classified the problem as critical, deciding to prepare a recall after identifying the safety risks associated with a detaching cushion.\nRather than replacing the entire head restraint or seat, service technicians are to apply two-component epoxy adhesive at three attachment points using the specified tools. Physical testing confirmed the improvement in attachment strength, and truth be told, some epoxy adhesive here and there is the best-case scenario for Volvo from the standpoint of repair costs.\nYV12YEL03V2615920 is featured in the recall documentation as the last vehicle built with the old cushion design. Volvo retailers have already been instructed, whereas customer notifications are in the offing for November 30, 2026. Affected vehicle identification numbers are now searchable on the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration's website.\nCurrently the smallest electric Volvo sport utility vehicle of them all, the EX30 is a strict five-seater that can be had in single- and twin-motor flavors. To be discontinued in the US market after model year 2026 due to tariffs, poor demand, the loss of incentives, and a general feeling of uncertainty, the EX40's brother now retails at $40,345 for its most basic of specs.\nHighlights include up to 422 horsepower, 3.4 seconds to 60 mph (97 kph), less than half an hour to recharge from 10 to 80 percent, and 261 miles (420 kilometers) on a full charge. The Cross Country version of the EX30 is ruggedized, targeting buyers who need additional ground clearance to suit their outdoorsy lifestyles.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nrecall volvo ex30 Volvo EV US\nDownload: Volvo EX30 headrest cushion recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1968 Mustang GT 390 Time Capsule Surfaces With Rarest Factory Option, Original Owner Had It 57 Years",
